{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793382723,"nonce":"Fig3q8j5JWvztxKcYLbQkXplMbs=","title":"GPU-enabled cloud session class: isolation backend, headless GPU rendering, video encode, entitlement and metering","message":"A paid session class whose guest has a GPU, so framework apps (Heaps, OpenFL, Flixel, Lime, Ceramic) render on it and stream to the browser or desktop through the surface protocol. Browser builds remain the default preview; this is the offering for native-fidelity and heavy rendering.\n\nOpen design points:\n- Isolation: MSB 0.7.11 has no GPU option on msb run. Options: GPU passthrough (VFIO) into a microVM, a different runner backend, or per-session containers with a GPU runtime on dedicated nodes. Needs a decision with whoever owns native/runner.\n- Rendering: EGL headless on the GPU, no display server.\n- Transport: hardware video encode (NVENC, VAAPI, QSV); extend the protocol's encoding with a video codec and stream chunks; present with ashui.media.Player. Keep damage rectangles for the CPU class.\n- Entitlement: add a session class (cpu, gpu) to rayde-sync Entitlement and Limits, with GPU-minute metering separate from storage quota; lease and expiry rules apply per class.\n- Cloud sync: a restored workspace must start in either class from the same snapshot.\n- Capacity and cost: scheduling, idle shutdown and abuse limits for scarce GPU nodes.\n\nRelates to 9c6f60e (framework previews), 784b568 (headless rendering in compute), 50df72f (frame transport and codec), e003324 (Cloud Sync).","files":null}]}